, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. Mandatory neutrality review: geography/civics only, with no historical narrative, pre-colonial kingdom, colonial content, migration grievance, conflict zone, ethnic overlay, resource-ownership claim, or implication that resources follow exact hard boundaries.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Sahel, Filayen ciyawa, Dazuzzuka, Gabar teku, Kogin Neja, Kogin Binuwai, Lokoja\n\n---\n\n### 19. `p4-socs-04` — National Identity — Our Flag, Anthem, Pledge, and Symbols\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-04.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-04.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. A respectful study sheet with an accurate Nigerian green-white-green flag, a verified coat of arms, a generic musical-note panel for the national anthem, a speech panel for the national pledge, and a diverse pupil group standing calmly with equal prominence. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental